# Converted from corpus/flowcharts/0137.mmd by corpus/convert.py.
Client (User A) -> Server (User B): Send RSA Public Key
Server (User B) -> Client (User A): Send RSA Public Key
Server (User B) -> Server (User B): Generate Kyber keypair
Server (User B) -> Client (User A): Send Kyber Public Key
Client (User A) -> Server (User B): Send RSA-encrypted AES Key
Client (User A) -> Server (User B): Send Kyber ciphertext / shared secret
Server (User B) -> Server (User B): Decapsulate Kyber / Decrypt AES Key
Server (User B) -> Server (User B): Derive final AES Key
Client (User A) -> Server (User B): Encrypt message with AES-GCM
Server (User B) -> Server (User B): Decrypt message with AES-GCM
Server (User B) -> Client (User A): Optional Reply AES-GCM
Start -> Generate Client RSA Keypair
Generate Client RSA Keypair -> Send Client RSA Public Key
Send Client RSA Public Key -> Receive Server RSA Public Key
Receive Server RSA Public Key -> Receive Server Kyber Public Key
Receive Server Kyber Public Key -> Generate Classical AES Key
Generate Classical AES Key -> Encrypt AES Key with Server RSA
Encrypt AES Key with Server RSA -> Send RSA-encrypted AES Key
Send RSA-encrypted AES Key -> Encapsulate Kyber shared secret
Encapsulate Kyber shared secret -> Send Kyber ciphertext / shared secret
Send Kyber ciphertext / shared secret -> Derive final AES Key (SHA256)
Derive final AES Key (SHA256) -> AES-GCM Encrypt Message
AES-GCM Encrypt Message -> Send Encrypted Message
Send Encrypted Message -> Server Receives Message
Server Receives Message -> Decrypt AES Key (RSA)
Decrypt AES Key (RSA) -> Decapsulate Kyber / Derive Shared Secret
Decapsulate Kyber / Derive Shared Secret -> Derive final AES Key (SHA256)
Derive final AES Key (SHA256) -> AES-GCM Decrypt Message
AES-GCM Decrypt Message -> Optional Reply with AES-GCM
Optional Reply with AES-GCM -> End
